<%- include('../partials/layout-start') %>
<%- include('../partials/page-header', {
  icon: 'bi-info-circle',
  heading: `About ${appName}`,
  sub: 'A marketplace for the SAP ecosystem — and a short account of what it will not do.'
}) %>
<section class="glass-section pb-5">
<div class="container">
  <div class="row justify-content-center"><div class="col-lg-8">

    <div class="glass-card mb-4"><div class="card-body">
      <h2 class="h5">What this is</h2>
      <p>
        <%= appName %> is where SAP work and the people who do it find each other. Companies
        and agencies advertise roles; consultants keep a profile describing what they have
        actually delivered; and both sides can see what the work is worth before the
        conversation about money starts.
      </p>
      <p class="mb-0">
        It is built around SAP's own vocabulary rather than a generic one. Modules are SAP
        modules, phases are <a href="/jobs">SAP Activate's</a>, and a role means what it
        means on a programme. That sounds like a detail until you search for FI and are
        shown everything containing the word "specific".
      </p>
    </div></div>

    <div class="glass-card mb-4"><div class="card-body">
      <h2 class="h5">What you can do here</h2>
      <ul class="mb-0">
        <li><strong><a href="/jobs">Find work</a></strong> — filtered by module, Activate
            phase, country and day rate, not by keyword alone.</li>
        <li><strong><a href="/consultants">Find consultants</a></strong> — searchable by the
            modules somebody has delivered on a real engagement, which is a different claim
            from a module ticked on a skills list.</li>
        <li><strong><a href="/rates">See what the work pays</a></strong> — an index built
            from figures contributed by members, published only where enough people have
            contributed to a bucket for it to mean anything.</li>
        <li><strong><a href="/quotes/new">Size a programme</a></strong> — an estimator that
            turns a scope into consultant-days, a phase plan and a budget you can hand to a
            client, with every figure reconciling against the total.</li>
        <li><strong><a href="/community">Ask the people who have done it</a></strong> — a
            community organised by the same product lines as everything else.</li>
      </ul>
    </div></div>

    <div class="glass-card mb-4"><div class="card-body">
      <h2 class="h5">What it deliberately does not do</h2>
      <p>
        These are decisions, not gaps, and they are listed because every one of them is
        something a site like this is routinely expected to have.
      </p>
      <ul class="mb-0">
        <li><strong>It does not calculate your tax savings.</strong> What you keep depends
            on where you live, how you are engaged and what year it is. A number on a page
            that does not know any of those is a financial claim we cannot stand behind.</li>
        <li><strong>It does not publish a rate derived from one person.</strong> Below the
            floor, the <a href="/rates">index</a> shows how many people contributed and
            withholds the figures. A sparse bucket is reported as a gap, never as a zero and
            never as a line drawn between two other points.</li>
        <li><strong>It does not let a browser decide a score.</strong> The
            <a href="/challenges">daily challenge</a> is graded on the server, from the
            answers you chose, because a leaderboard anybody can post a perfect score to
            devalues everybody else's.</li>
        <li><strong>It does not delete what it moderates.</strong> A post taken down is
            hidden, with who did it and why recorded, so the decision can be looked at
            again — and reversed.</li>
        <li><strong>It does not message anyone on your behalf.</strong> Nothing here sends
            an introduction, a follow-up or a pitch unless a person pressed send.</li>
      </ul>
    </div></div>

    <div class="glass-card mb-4"><div class="card-body">
      <h2 class="h5">About the figures</h2>
      <p class="mb-0">
        A quote keeps the breakdown it was priced with, and the version of the catalogue
        behind it. If the underlying effort baselines later change, the quote is flagged as
        priced under an older catalogue — never silently re-priced. What was said to a
        client on a date stays what was said to them on that date. The
        <a href="/rates">rate index</a> works the same way round: contributed figures are
        never edited, only set aside, because an average whose inputs somebody can retype is
        an average nobody should trust.
      </p>
    </div></div>

    <div class="glass-card"><div class="card-body">
      <h2 class="h5">Independent of SAP SE</h2>
      <p class="mb-0">
        <%= appName %> is not affiliated with, endorsed by or sponsored by SAP SE. SAP and
        the product names used throughout the site are trademarks of SAP SE, used here to
        describe the work. Questions go to <a href="/contact">a person</a>.
      </p>
    </div></div>

  </div></div>
</div>
</section>
<%- include('../partials/layout-end') %>
